    "result_text": "The build is green. Here's what I implemented for the `customerCode` feature:\n\n## Summary\n\nThe requirement: on owner creation, assign `customerCode` formatted `<LAST3>-<NNNN>` \u2014 `LAST3` = upper-cased first three letters of `lastName`, `NNNN` = a global 4-digit zero-padded sequence equal to one more than the current owner count (e.g. `SMI-0007`).\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`Owner` entity** (`model/Owner.java`) \u2014 added a `customerCode` field mapped to a `customer_code` column, plus getter/setter.\n\n2. **Schema** \u2014 added `customer_code` to the `owners` table in all four DB schemas (`hsqldb`, `h2`, `mysql`, `postgres`). The acceptance test runs on the `hsqldb` profile with `ddl-auto=none`, so the schema.sql change is what makes the column exist.\n\n3. **OpenAPI** (`openapi.yml`) \u2014 added a `readOnly` `customerCode` property to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries the field and MapStruct maps it by name (no mapper change needed).\n\n4. **New OfficeFloor function** `AssignCustomerCode` \u2014 reads the built `@Val Owner`, computes `LAST3` (guarding against short names) and `count + 1` via `ownerRepository.findAll().size()`, and sets the code with `String.format(\"%s-%04d\", \u2026)`.\n\n5. **Pipeline wiring** (`owners.POST.yml`) \u2014 inserted an `assignCode` step between `build` and `save`, under `govern: [ transaction ]`, so the code is assigned in-place on the entity before it's persisted.\n\nBecause the count is read before the new owner is saved, two consecutive creates produce consecutive sequence numbers, satisfying the test's `Integer.parseInt(ca\u2026)+1 == Integer.parseInt(cb\u2026)` assertion. Full suite: **Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0 \u2014 BUILD SUCCESS.**",
